<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auld Alliance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1295">1295</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Franco-Scottish alliance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Auld Alliance was the alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, first sealed by a treaty of 1295 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a> and Philip IV and renewed by most later Scottish kings. It drew Scotland into repeated wars with England, including the invasion that ended at Flodden in 1513, and brought French troops and influence into Scotland. It effectively ended with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of 1560.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auld-alliance.jpg" alt="Auld Alliance" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joan Blaeu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the death of the seven-year-old Margaret, the Maid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, in 1290, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> moved to extend his authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The Council of Twelve, which governed Scotland for the time being, looked for allies abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was a natural partner, since Philip IV had declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> forfeit in 1294. A Scottish embassy agreed the Treaty of Paris, signed on 23 October 1295.</p><p>Like every later renewal, the treaty favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The French had only to continue their struggle against the English in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, while the cost of any war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> fell entirely on the Scots. Scotland was nonetheless allied to a major European power. The treaty gave no protection against Edward's invasion of 1296, and the Scots owed their survival chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> and to the mistakes of Edward II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The alliance took on urgency after 1332, when Edward III set out to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and press his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1334 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ii-of-scotland">David II of Scotland</a> and his court found refuge in France, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-gaillard">Château Gaillard</a>, and the French paid an annual pension of £2000 for their upkeep. In 1346 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at Philip VI's urging and was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Battle of Neville's Cross</a>.</p><p>In 1418 the Dauphin, the future Charles VII, called on Scottish help against Henry V, and as many as 15,000 Scottish troops were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between 1419 and 1424. French and Scottish forces won at the Battle of Baugé in 1421 but were defeated at Verneuil in 1424. Scots fought beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> in 1429 and served in the Garde Écossaise, the bodyguard of the French crown.</p><p>The alliance was renewed in 1512, 1517 and 1548, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> lost James IV and most of his nobles at Flodden in 1513. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> spread in Scotland, more people favoured links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> of 1560 ended the formal ties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The alliance shaped Scottish life beyond war. Scots studied at French universities, and there were reciprocal dual nationality agreements, French privileges for Scottish vintners, and an influence on architecture, law and the Scots language.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> called it the oldest alliance in the world in a speech at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> in June 1942. Both countries marked its 700th anniversary in 1995. The historian Siobhan Talbott concluded that it had never been formally revoked.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auld Alliance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The alliance with France, the <b>Auld Alliance</b> first sealed in 1295, drew Scotland into repeated wars with England: James IV built a royal navy, including the great ship Michael, but was killed with much of his nobility at the Battle of Flodden in 1513, and another heavy defeat…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty between John Balliol of Scotland and Philip IV of France, signed in Paris, formed the <b>Auld Alliance</b> against England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
